    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "math:radical-equations",
        "cnm_079"
      ],
      "explanation": "The square root is real only when \\(x\\ge2\\). Since it is nonnegative, the equation also requires \\(x\\le4\\). Notice that \\(x=3\\) works:\n\\[\n3+\\sqrt{3-2}=3+1=4.\n\\]\nTo determine whether another root is possible, compare both terms with their values at \\(x=3\\).\n\nIf \\(2\\le x<3\\), then \\(\\sqrt{x-2}<1\\), so\n\\[\nx+\\sqrt{x-2}<3+1=4.\n\\]\nIf \\(3<x\\le4\\), then \\(\\sqrt{x-2}>1\\), so\n\\[\nx+\\sqrt{x-2}>3+1=4.\n\\]\nThese cases cover every other allowed value, including the endpoints. Thus \\(x=3\\) is the only real root, and the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
      "id": "compare-with-three",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1950_AMC_12_Q24#compare-with-three",
      "title": "Compare possible roots with 3"
    },
